{
  "result": "At the edge of town, a small library stood between a bakery and an empty lot. Its windows were uneven, its carpet had faded, and its front door complained whenever anyone entered. Yet every morning, people arrived carrying questions they had not quite learned how to ask themselves in private.\n\nThe librarian, Mara, believed that books were less like doors than bridges. Doors suggested a choice between inside and outside; bridges admitted that you could belong to both shores. She recommended atlases to restless children, poems to busy parents, and gardening manuals to anyone recovering from a long, difficult year.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, a boy brought her a stone. He said it had come from the empty lot, where weeds grew through the cracked pavement. He wanted to know whether it was valuable. Mara examined its ordinary gray surface and asked what he thought it might be worth to him.\n\nHe considered this carefully. The stone had fit his palm, survived a storm, and made him curious enough to visit the library. Perhaps, he decided, it was worth a story. Together they invented a history involving a fallen moon, an unusually patient fox, and a king who feared deep puddles.\n\nWhen the rain stopped, the boy carried the stone home. Mara returned to her desk, where a stack of books waited to be shelved. Outside, sunlight touched the vacant lot, turning each puddle into a small, bright window. For a moment, the whole town seemed ready to be imagined anew.",
